### POST BODY

Hi all, I've recently finished an ML based Bazaar tool that predicts entry times for trades with items in the Bazaar.
I tested it a few weeks ago and was able to make ~30 mil in about 15 minutes from it accurately predicting a price spike for summoning eyes (doing some backend testing its like 95% accurate on price spikes iirc). I currently only have it running for 21 high demand items, but I could in theory train a model for every item (the main limitation right now is training time).
I have data scraped from up to ~November, and I’ll probably update that later. Let me know if anyone would be interested in seeing the data I currently have or the list of items the model is trained on.
If anyone is interested in how it works, I basically scrape historical Bazaar data from CoffleNet using proxy servers, then process that data to label good trade entry points (defined as times when the price is expected to go up). I use a regressor instead of a classifier because I’m interested not just in predicting when the price will go up, but also by how much. The model trains on these labels and can then predict and rank trade entry times across different items. The idea is to optimize Bazaar flipping by highlighting items that are statistically likely to see increases in sell offer price over time (you get to profitable flips before anyone else does). There’s also some basic risk management involved and a bunch of other stuff I could go into more detail in if anyone is interested.
From the testing I’ve done, this seems like it could be a strong Bazaar analysis tool for people who already flip or want to understand price movements better. I also made a frontend mod integration (1.8.9 only) that provides a cleaner visual for trade entries instead of reading raw JSON output. It isn’t included in the GitHub yet, but I can share it later once a few things are cleaned up.
TLDR: ML-based Bazaar trade entry prediction tool, 100m+/hr money making method.

### COMMENT REPLIES

The first part was getting all the data which was entirely done through prompting cofflenet, their data aggregation is really weird though so I had to prompt their api for one day intervals for a consistent 5 minute spacing between data points. The issue with that was then I ran into rate limits a bunch which is where I started using proxy servers. With the servers it still took ~2 hours iirc to get 700k data points for all 1417 items in the game. Their mayor data was also super weird to parse because it had so many inconsistencies.
After I had all the data the new issue was how do I label it and what do I want the model to predict. Initially I had it predicting future buy/sell prices, and while that was helpful for predicting good long term investments, I wanted the overall project to be useful for bazaar flipping on a day to day basis. I then experimented with some other weird labeling logics until I decided it would probably be best to enforce some sort of interval based logic. My idea was look through the data, find profitable time intervals, mark those intervals for "trades." After some research I tried doing some sort of stop loss versus take profit system where basically you start from some data point, define some percentage for how much your willing to los and how much you want to gain, whichever is reached first is what that entry point is labeled with. The issue I found with this is the model tended to overfit, and sl tp was also too arbitrary for something useful. After that was when I decided on the labeling logic I would actually use where for each data point you look one day in the future find the max and min of the price. From there we label the future return as follows. If the min is larger than the max, immediately label the future return as the min, this is to stop extremely lossy events where you might miss a max, if the min is less than the max now you look at which happens first. If the max happens first, your future return is the max, if the min happens first the future return is the min. What this does is it creates like a risk aware suggestion logic where if you are to buy an item at that entry price, you are generally expected to be able to flip it for increasingly more money, it is then the duty of the trader to figure out when to stop and that is where u can implement a tp trading mentality, i.e. lets say the model predicts the item to go up 20% at its peak, u keep trading until it reaches 18% or something of that like.
So after I finally had the whole labelling logic figured out I had to actually train the model. Initially I was gonna train off all 1417 items, but I don't really have a PC to run ML on rn, so I had to shorten it to a list of only 21 items and even then it was an annoyingly long training time. I then ran some testing with train val data, thought it was good enough, and then trained off my 21 item list. After that was done I did some in game testing and it was still very accurate (the summoning eye one is the only trade I actually entered but I tracked some other items and saw the predicted spikes happen one such example was control switch spiking from 180k to 202k.
So thats basically like the entire logic process I went through while making the project.

